1026	A final feature of the compressed instruction set has to do with load and
1027	store instructions.  All CIS load and store instructions use the form
1028	{\tt Rb+\#Imm}.  The instruction encoding that would otherwise be for
1029	an {\tt \#Imm} alone has been made into a shorthand for using the stack pointer
1030	as {\tt Rb} with an offset.  Hence the compressed instruction set allows loads
1031	and stores to offsets of the Stack Pointer of -128~octets on up to~127 octets.
1032	In practice, this gives the compressed load and store instructions, when
1033	referencing the stack, thirty--two words that they can reference.
1034	
1035	This compressed instruction set is somewhat similar to other architectures that
1036	have a thumb instruction set, with the difference that the ZipCPU can intermix
1037	regular and compressed instructions at will.  When using the CIS, instructions
1038	are still issued one at a time, however interrupts are disabled between
1039	instruction halves in order to prevent the CPU from stopping and then needing
1040	to re-start mid-instruction.  Further, it is the silent job of the assembler
1041	to generate CIS instructions in an opportunistic fashion--unless this feature
1042	has been disabled on the command line.
1043	
1044	The disassembler represents CIS instructions by placing a vertical bar
1045	between the two components, while still leaving them on the same line.
1046	
